 "reading": {
  "problem": "A run had two worker pools: the tensor pool, sized by `-t`, and the library GEMM's own pool, which OpenBLAS creates in a constructor at load at the machine's thread count (31 threads on this host), before any API call can reach it. On a loaded host the two compete: the uncapped library loses to the canonical kernel from four threads up (2026-10-23-gemm-thread-budget-linux.json), and the pool's workers spin between calls even when the library is not used, on the canonical path too.",
  "change": "The library runs one thread for every call; the tensor pool keeps the run's thread count. camel::initialize() seeds OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS and MKL_NUM_THREADS to 1 when the user has not set them, before any module is loaded, which removes the constructor's pool. gemm_threads() reports what a call got: 1 for a library call, the pool's width for a canonical call. A new process.thread_count() and test/cases/modules/tensor/thread_pools.cml pin the process's thread count within `-t`, so a second pool is red.",
  "curve_says": "Ten interleaved rounds, two copies of each build, a null control per cell. Removing the second pool helps where it was pure overhead: the batched 4x1024^3 library cell at -t 16 falls from 80.4 to 57.4 ms (-29%, null 13%), the canonical 2048x1024x1024 at -t 4 from 44.7 to 31.5 (-29%, null 9%), the canonical 4x1024^3 at -t 4 from 70.5 to 58.5 (-17%, null 6%). It costs the library's own whole-work products at four threads and up: 2048x1024x1024 is 53.5 -> 86.5 ms at 4 (+62%), 43.1 -> 87.4 at 8 (+103%), 52.9 -> 89.2 at 16 (+69%); 2048x1024x2048 is 85.9 -> 165.7 (+93%), 59.0 -> 170.8 (+189%), 62.2 -> 157.7 (+154%). At 1024^3 the two are level (39.8 -> 43.2 at 4, 36.0 -> 40.6 at 8, 46.4 -> 42.8 at 16: the wide library was already no faster there). The rest of the read cells are small (512x256x512 and the 4-D attention product are 1-2 ms, with nulls of the same order) or the same pattern at t=2 and t=32; all 84 cells carry their nulls and a resolvable flag.",
  "step_says": "Not resolved: the d=1024 step at -t 16 measured at loads 129-180; the two copies read library 5750/4248 ms before and 5607/5137 after (mean change +8%, null 35%), and canonical 5141/2680 before and 3300/3127 after (-18%, null 92%). The before copies disagree by more than the change, so the step is unresolved; the after copies agree with each other, which is the one-pool effect this host can show without a quiet window: the variance falls, the median does not move beyond the null.",
  "threads": "Import `tensor`, -t 1: 32 threads (31 library + caller) before, 1 after. -t 16 canonical product: 47 before, 16 after. -t 16 batched product: 47 before, 16 after. -t 16 library product: 32 before, 1 after. The library's pool is created at load and never joined; its 31 threads cost about 1 MB of RSS (import 20.5-21.0 MB -> 20.0-20.2 MB; 1024^3 product 35.6 -> 34.6 MB), i.e. threads and cores, not memory. A user-set OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS is left alone and re-creates the pool, so the single-pool promise is conditional on it being unset.",
  "licence": "Canonical: all 42 cells (six thread counts, seven shapes) carry identical checksums before and after; #strict and safe_fp withhold the library. Library: before, each whole-work shape had six distinct checksums across t = 1..32 (OpenBLAS groups partial sums by worker); after, one per shape at every -t, equal to the old t=1 checksum. The library path already ran under the default reassociation licence; the change removes orders rather than adding one, and the seed touches no sum.",
  "unresolved": "The d=1024 step is unresolved at this load (null 35% and 92%); the curve's 1024^3 cell and the step are consistent with a wash on the library variant, but that is not a resolved result. A user-set OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS or MKL_NUM_THREADS re-introduces the second pool; the guarantee is conditional.",
  "next": "The library at one thread is 1.6-2.9x slower than the pool it replaced on the wide whole-work products, so the follow-up is to split one whole-work library product over the tensor pool by output rows and call the library one thread per chunk: one pool, the library's parallelism kept, no idle threads. That is a change in matmul's library branch, not in the thread setting; the seed and the one-thread rule stay. Not implemented here."
 },
